A prefix ++/-- target is an assignment target.

postfix_expr's deferred dereference loop carries BOTH orderings:

    if(!assigning) { current_target = current_target->type;   /* step */
                     load at current_target->size; }          /* pointed-to width */
    else           { load at current_target->size;            /* POINTER width */
                     current_target = current_target->type; } /* step */

The second is correct for a destination -- its own comment says "Load the
POINTER (this level's width)" -- and `--*p` took the first, because the test
only asked whether the next token was `=`.

WHILE EVERY TYPE WAS REGISTER-WIDTH BOTH WIDTHS WERE EIGHT and the branches
were interchangeable. At four bytes `--*p` loads an eight-byte pointer with a
four-byte ldrsw and then increments through the truncated address:

    int=8:  ldr_x0,[x0]      reads p, 8 bytes
    int=4:  ldrsw_x0,[x0]    reads p, 4 bytes

dynarray_reset's loop is `for (p = *pp; *n; ++p, --*n)`, so the tcc micro-c
built segfaulted before it had parsed anything. `--version` died.

prefix_lvalue_target is already set by the prefix handler for exactly this
purpose and is still standing at this point: the member sites at 2051 and 2270
consume it, and `--*p` reaches neither.

HOW IT WAS FOUND. Two structural hypotheses failed first -- one of them
regressed cases 17, 23 and 28 and was reverted. What settled it was tagging all
22 load_value call sites with markers, compiling `--*p`, and reading which
marker came out. That is the same lesson as zzx: measure which code runs,
do not reason about which code should run. This file has FIVE near-duplicate
copies of this dereference rule and its own comments count them off; reading
them is how both wrong hypotheses were formed.

MEASURED: difftest stays 84/85 -- no regression -- and mc-tcc stops crashing at
startup. `--version` returns 0 where it segfaulted before.

STILL BROKEN: `(*p)--`, the parenthesised postfix form, takes a different path
and still faults. 14-function-pointer-member still faults. mc-tcc has not been
run through the twelve programs yet.

